### When do you use each executor?

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class ExecutorChoiceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Fixed: steady stream of trade settlements
        ExecutorService fixed = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(16);

        // Cached: burst of SMS notifications
        ExecutorService cached = Executors.newCachedThreadPool();

        // Scheduled: daily interest posting
        ScheduledExecutorService scheduled = Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(2);
        scheduled.scheduleAtFixedRate(
                () -> System.out.println("Posting interest"),
                0, 24, TimeUnit.HOURS);

        // ForkJoinPool: sum a million transaction amounts
        ForkJoinPool fjp = new ForkJoinPool();
    }
}
```

### Why is ForkJoinPool poor for blocking IO?

ForkJoinPool is sized to roughly one thread per core, assuming threads are always running. If a worker blocks on a database or HTTP call, that core sits idle. Other workers cannot steal from the blocked worker, and the pool does not automatically grow to compensate (unless ManagedBlocker is used, which is unreliable for bulk IO).

### How do you compare them?

| Executor | Best for | Danger | Production advice |
|---|---|---|---|
| Fixed | Steady bounded load | Unbounded queue OOM | Use ThreadPoolExecutor with bounded queue |
| Cached | Short bursty tasks | Unbounded threads | Never for slow/blocking tasks |
| Scheduled | Timed/periodic | Unbounded queue | Fine for most uses |
| ForkJoinPool | CPU recursion | IO blocks all workers | Never for blocking IO |

### What is the difference between scheduleAtFixedRate and scheduleWithFixedDelay?

scheduleAtFixedRate: runs every N time units measured from the start of each run. If a run takes longer than N, the next run starts immediately after (runs can bunch up). scheduleWithFixedDelay: waits N time units after each run completes before starting the next. Runs never overlap.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can you change the ForkJoinPool common pool size? Yes, with the system property `-Djava.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool.common.parallelism=N`, but this is a tuning aid, not a substitute for keeping IO off the common pool.
- Should I ever use Executors factories in production? Prefer ThreadPoolExecutor directly for fixed pools (bounded queue). Scheduled is acceptable as-is.
